# Token-Efficient Hudbird Workflow

- Start with `docs/PROJECT_CONTEXT.md`, then the relevant part of `docs/ARCHITECTURE_MAP.md` and `docs/WORKLOG.md`. Do not reopen long source documents unless these summaries are insufficient.
- Default to one clearly bounded outcome per iteration.
- Before editing, inspect only the files directly involved. Do not inventory the whole monorepo unless required.
- Reuse prior findings and existing components; do not repeat completed discovery or visual research.
- Use the smallest relevant skill set. Do not load overlapping design skills unless the user explicitly requests a multi-skill audit.
- Separate implementation, broad QA, documentation, registry work, and audit into distinct iterations unless the user explicitly asks for an end-to-end release.
- During implementation, run focused typechecks and tests first. Run the full delivery gate only when the user asks to finalize or release.
- Perform browser review only at representative desktop and mobile widths during iteration. Check every breakpoint and theme only at the final delivery gate.
- Do not create screenshots, generated proof assets, compatibility layers, or new public APIs unless they are part of the requested outcome.
- Preserve existing APIs by default, but do not expand scope to compatibility work without evidence that the edited code affects them.
- Send short progress updates. Report outcomes, blockers, and decisions; omit routine command narration.
- If a request would touch more than one package or combines more than three deliverables, state the proposed slices and implement only the first approved slice.
- Stop after the requested slice is working. Offer the next slice instead of beginning it automatically.
- Never trade correctness, accessibility, or preservation of user work for token savings.

## Default Iteration

1. Confirm the single outcome in one sentence.
2. Inspect targeted files.
3. Implement the smallest complete change.
4. Run focused verification.
5. Summarize what changed and propose one next step.

Full audits, exhaustive browser matrices, packed CLI consumer tests, and monorepo-wide validation belong to a named finalization iteration.
